    <!-- Unit Overview -->
    <section class="welcome-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-compass section-icon"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
        <p>The focus shifts from single-class logic to System Architecture. Students explore how to build extensible and maintainable software through class hierarchies and structured relationships. Key themes include Code Reuse (Inheritance), Dynamic Behaviour (Polymorphism), and Lifecycle Dependency (Composition).</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Syllabus Links -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-bookmark section-icon"></i> What You'll Learn</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.2.1</strong> — Relationship types: "Is-a" (Inheritance) vs. "Has-a" (Composition/Aggregation).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.2.2</strong> — Inheritance implementation: Parent/Superclass vs. Child/Subclass. Using super() to extend functionality.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.2.3</strong> — Polymorphism: Method overriding to provide specialized behaviour in subclasses.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.2.4</strong> — Abstraction: Using abstract base classes (ABCs) to define interfaces without implementation.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.2.5</strong> — Composition: Managing objects within objects where the "part" cannot exist without the "whole."
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.2.6</strong> — Design Patterns: Introduction to common solutions (e.g., Singleton or Factory) for architectural challenges.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

